    <!-- ManualPanel -->
    <string name="panel_manual_help">Direct manual shutter control with two modes.\n\n• HOLD: The shutter stays open as long as you press the button. Release to close. Ideal for quick bulb shots.\n• LOCK: Tap once to open the shutter, tap again to close. Best for long exposures where you don\u2019t want to hold the button.\n\nSwitch between Hold and Lock using the segmented buttons in the action area.</string>
    <string name="panel_manual_tip">Toggle behaviour using the chips in the action area below.</string>
    <string name="panel_manual_lock_info">LOCK: Tap the center button once to open the shutter, tap again to close. Best for long bulb exposures.</string>
    <string name="panel_manual_hold_info">HOLD: Shutter remains open as long as the center button is pressed. Ideal for quick bursts.</string>

    <!-- AstroPanel -->
    <string name="panel_astro_help">Calculate the longest exposure that keeps stars as sharp points instead of trails.\n\n• Choose your sensor preset (crop factor) and lens focal length.\n• 500 Rule: classic formula, generous — some trailing on modern sensors.\n• 400 Rule: tighter, better for high-resolution sensors.\n• NPF Rule: most accurate — uses pixel pitch and f/2.8 aperture for sharp stars.\n• Formula: Rule ÷ (focal length × crop factor) = max seconds.\n• Gap is the pause between exposures for the camera to write to card.\n• Adjust shot count and delay just like in Intervalometer mode.</string>
    <string name="label_npf_formula">NPF / (%1$d mm × %2$sx) = %3$ss</string>
    <string name="npf_estimation_note">ⓘ This is an estimation — tap for details</string>
    <string name="npf_dialog_title">NPF Rule Estimation</string>
    <string name="npf_dialog_body">The NPF rule calculates: (35 × aperture + 30 × pixel pitch) ÷ (focal length × crop factor).\n\nSince Pulsar doesn\'t know your camera\'s exact sensor specs, we estimate based on your crop factor:\n\n• Pixel pitch: %1$s μm (typical for ~24 MP sensors of this format)\n• Aperture: f/%2$s (common for astro lenses)\n\nThese assumptions work well for most modern cameras. For higher-resolution sensors (e.g. 45–61 MP full frame), the actual pixel pitch is smaller, meaning this estimate is slightly generous — you may see minor trailing.\n\nFor exact results, use an online NPF calculator with your specific camera and lens specs, then enter the computed exposure manually in Intervalometer mode.</string>
    <string name="label_effective_focal">%1$smm Effective</string>
    <string name="label_max_exposure">Max Exposure</string>
    <string name="label_astro_formula">Formula: %1$d / (%2$d mm × %3$sx) = %4$ss</string>
